{"id":"paris-gun","slug":"paris-gun","name":"Paris Gun","aliases":["Pariskanone","Paris Cannon","Wilhelm-Geschütz","Wilhelm-Geschutz","Wilhelm-Geschuetz","Kaiser Wilhelm Gun","Kaiser Wilhelm Geschütz","Kaiser Wilhelm Geschutz","Kaiser Wilhelm Geschuetz"],"kind":"ordnance","category":"artillery","subcategory":"German super heavy field gun of World War I","description":"The Paris Gun was a type of German long-range siege gun, several of which were used to bombard Paris during World War I. They were in service from March to August 1918. When the guns were first employed, Parisians believed they had been bombed by a high-altitude Zeppelin, as the sound of neither an airplane nor a gun could be heard. They were the largest pieces of artillery used during the war by barrel length, and qualify under the (later) formal definition of large-calibre artillery.\nAlso called the \"Kaiser Wilhelm Geschütz\", they were often confused with Big Bertha, the German howitzer used against Belgian forts in the Battle of Liège in 1914; indeed, the French called them by this name as well. They were also confused with the smaller \"Langer Max\" cannon, from which they were derived.","facts":[],"specifications":[{"group":"Cartridge & Action","items":[{"label":"Cartridge","value":"211 mm, later rebored to 238 mm","numeric":211,"unit":"mm"},{"label":"Muzzle Velocity","value":"1640 m/s","numeric":1640,"unit":"m/s"}]},{"group":"Dimensions","items":[{"label":"Weight","value":"256 t","numeric":256,"unit":"t"},{"label":"Length","value":"34 m84","numeric":34,"unit":"m"},{"label":"Barrel Length","value":"21 m","numeric":21,"unit":"m"}]},{"group":"Performance","items":[{"label":"Maximum Range","value":"130 km","numeric":130,"unit":"km"}]},{"group":"Production","items":[{"label":"Designer","value":"Dr. Fritz Rausenberger"},{"label":"Manufacturer","value":"Krupp"}]},{"group":"Service","items":[{"label":"Origin","value":"German Empire"}]}],"specIndex":{"Cartridge":"211 mm, later rebored to 238 mm","Muzzl